// In URP shaders, you access the Main Light like this:
// (This is what happens under the hood)

Light mainLight = GetMainLight();

// mainLight.color       → RGB color of the light
// mainLight.direction   → Direction the light shines (for directional)
// mainLight.distanceAttenuation → 1.0 for directional, falloff for point/spot
// mainLight.shadowAttenuation   → 0.0 (shadow) to 1.0 (fully lit)

// In a custom URP shader:
float3 N = normalize(normalWS);
float3 L = mainLight.direction;
float NdotL = saturate(dot(N, L));
float3 diffuse = albedo * mainLight.color * NdotL * mainLight.shadowAttenuation;

// URP Additional Light settings in the URP Asset:
//
// Lighting → Additional Lights:
//   - Per Vertex: lighting calculated at vertices, interpolated across triangles
//   - Per Pixel: lighting calculated per pixel (better quality, slower)
//
// Lighting → Max Additional Lights: 0 to 8 (default: 4)
//
// In a URP shader, Additional Lights are looped over:
//
// #pragma multi_compile _ _ADDITIONAL_LIGHTS_VERTEX _ADDITIONAL_LIGHTS
//
// This shader keyword controls whether Additional Lights are:
// - Off (no additional lights)
// - Per-Vertex (cheaper)
// - Per-Pixel (better quality)
//
// Unity automatically picks the right shader variant based on URP settings.

// Light Mode settings on a Light component:
//
// Realtime:     Light affects everything, shadows update every frame
// Mixed:        Direct light is real-time, indirect light is baked
// Baked:        Light is fully baked, no runtime cost, no dynamic shadows
//
// On the Mesh Renderer:
// Contribute Global Illumination = On → Object receives baked lighting
// Static = On → Object can be baked into lightmaps
//
// In URP shaders, baked lighting is accessed via:
// float3 bakedGI = SampleSH(normalWS); // Spherical Harmonics for indirect
